From 7a5229bd1721e1419e58e02d45d1034c4c103a87 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pctablet505 <pctablet505@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 23:48:00 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] [Qwen4Exp] Warm the Triton specialisations that JIT
 mid-inference

The JIT monitor reports seven Triton kernels compiling after the server is
ready, each stalling the request that hits it. Their launchers pick constexprs
from batch-shape buckets that the scheduler-driven warmup never reaches:

- qsa_sparse_paged_attention buckets (BLOCK_N, num_splits, num_warps) on
  num_rows * num_kv_heads, but warmup_kernels() prompts each warmup request
  with decode_query_len + 1 tokens, so its batches top out around 80 rows and
  the two largest buckets are never compiled. _qsa_merge_splitk_kernel keys on
  the resulting NUM_SPLITS, so it misses the same buckets.
- _qsa_mqa_paged_kernel flips TILES_PER_PROG at 32 rows, which a small
  max_num_seqs never crosses.
- The sampler warms with SamplingParams.for_sampler_warmup(), which sets
  top_k/top_p and therefore takes the FlashInfer branch; a default API request
  sets neither and takes the Triton Gumbel branch instead.
- Triton additionally specialises every runtime integer into one of three
  classes (== 1, divisible by 16, otherwise), so hitting a bucket at one
  divisibility class still leaves a compile for serving.

Factor the launch profiles in ops/qsa.py into pure functions, enumerate the
reachable specialisation set from the running config, and compile it during
kernel_warmup(). The driver launches with request ids, page tables and
selection indices set to -1, which every QSA kernel treats as invisible, so it
compiles the exact binaries serving uses without reading a KV byte.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
